VBR rework (unreleased)
=======================

-q is now a quality scale, not a raw quantizer value
----------------------------------------------------

-q used to expose libfaac's internal quantizer precision directly: range
10-5000, of which most sits past transparency, unevenly spaced, and documented
as a "percent" it never was. It is now a small readable scale, 0 to 10, higher
is better. The raw knob is still reachable as --quantqual, which takes exactly
the values -q used to take and produces byte-identical output.

Where a default is needed -- the library, and the GUI when VBR is selected with
the field left empty -- it is 4, the step closest to what the old default
quantizer setting produced. Passing no rate option at all still means ABR at
128 kbps, unchanged.

*** -q 10 has flipped meaning. *** It is legal in both the old range and the
new one: it used to be near the worst quality and is now the best. Every other
old value (100, 150, 500, ...) falls outside 0-10 and is now rejected with an
error naming --quantqual, so this is the one legacy invocation that will
silently do something different. Check your scripts for it.

Steps are spaced evenly in bitrate across the range the encoder can actually
produce, so one step means one quality whatever the sample rate and channel
count. Typical whole-stream rates for 48 kHz stereo:

  -q 0  ~27    -q 3  ~76    -q 6  ~218   -q 9  ~445
  -q 1  ~37    -q 4  ~100   -q 7  ~300   -q 10 ~512
  -q 2  ~52    -q 5  ~152   -q 8  ~372   (kbps)

These are typical values measured over a mixed corpus, not targets. Constant
quality means the bitrate follows the material: a dense track costs more than a
sparse one at the same setting. Use -b when the size is what matters.

VBR reaches the bitrates it claims
----------------------------------

The quality<->rate map was fitted years ago as a starting seed for ABR's
feedback loop, which corrects its own error. VBR has no loop, so it needed the
map to be a real model and it was not one -- at 48 kHz stereo it was around 70%
off. It has been refitted from measured output as a power law, total_kbps =
C * q^k, with C derived from channel count and sample rate rather than a table
of hand-fitted constants.

One consequence was a band of bitrates no -q value could reach. Adjacent -q
steps could step the output rate by ~110% across the AAC-LC/HE-AAC AUTO switch,
because -q meant a different thing on each side of it. -q is now normalised
across the switch and the largest step is 3-10%.

Measured against the previous release over a 49-clip corpus, bitrate-adjusted:
VBR +0.0399 MOS, ABR +0.0120 MOS.

AUTO picks the profile by bandwidth, not by bitrate
---------------------------------------------------

--object-type auto compared the requested bitrate against a hardcoded window
with a hand-fitted sample-rate ramp. Those numbers were measured before several
rounds of improvement to both the LC and HE-AAC paths and had drifted.

The choice is now made from the bandwidth the AAC-LC core would actually code at
the operating point: well short of Nyquist, SBR has real work to do and HE-AAC
v1 wins; close to it, SBR only adds artefacts to a band LC already codes
properly. One dimensionless threshold replaces the whole window, and it tracks
the encoder instead of a snapshot of it.

HE-AAC v1 is limited to mono and stereo in this encoder
-------------------------------------------------------

FAAC emits one SBR payload per frame, and the decoder binds it to the element it
follows, so it can serve a single SCE or CPE. Above two channels the remaining
elements got no SBR at all, and 5.1 was worse than that: the payload landed
against the LFE element, which decoders reject outright ("cannot apply SBR to
element type 3"). Encoding 5.1 through --object-type auto at a low enough rate
produced a stream ffmpeg would not play.

*** If you encoded multichannel with auto at low rates on an earlier release,
those files are affected. *** Depending on the channel layout you got either an
invalid stream or one in which only two channels had their top octave rebuilt.
Re-encoding is the fix; there is nothing that can repair the existing files.

AUTO no longer resolves to HE-AAC v1 above two channels, and asking for it
explicitly there now fails to open rather than emitting a broken stream. You get
AAC-LC instead, which is correct.

This is an implementation limit, not a limit of the format. MPEG-4 carries
sbr_extension_data() in a fill element after each channel element, so
multichannel HE-AAC v1 is legal and widely used; FAAC just does not emit a
payload per element yet. Doing so -- and correctly emitting none for the LFE,
which the format does exclude from SBR -- is what would lift the restriction.

-b bounds are enforced, and correctly
-------------------------------------

MaxBitrate() was read as per-channel in one place and as a whole-stream rate in
another, so the top half of the format's range was silently unreachable: -b 400
and -b 576 both emitted 294 kbps at 48 kHz stereo without a word. MinBitrate()
had no callers at all, so -b 2 was accepted and quietly produced 16.9 kbps.

-b is now clamped at both ends, and out-of-range requests clamp rather than
fail: -b 580 yields the maximum instead of "Couldn't open encoder instance". The
ceiling is the normative one, ISO/IEC 14496-3's 6144 bits per channel per frame;
the 8 kbps/channel floor is FAAC's own judgement about the lowest request worth
attempting, not a requirement of the format.

The underlying defect was that a bitrate carried either a per-channel or a
whole-stream value with nothing in the name saying which. Every such quantity
now states its unit.

A request can also go unmet for reasons no up-front check can see: the content
saturates the quantizer before the budget is spent, or the rate sits under what
the per-frame overhead costs at that frame rate (a 96 kHz stream runs twice the
frames of a 48 kHz one, each with its own headers, so a very low -b there asks
for less than the frames cost). The encoder does not chase such a request below
its own quality floor -- that trades real quality for a target it would still
miss. It reports instead, whenever the delivered average misses the request by
more than 15%:

  delivered 30 kbps against the 16 kbps requested: the encoder cannot go lower
  at this sample rate and channel count

API changes
-----------

  faac_params.bit_rate       -> bit_rate_per_ch
  faac_params.max_bit_rate   -> max_bit_rate_total

These are renames only. The struct layout is unchanged, so this is a source
break, not an ABI break -- a rebuild against the new header is all that is
needed, and the compiler points at every site.

  faac_params.vbr_quality    (new)

The 0-10 scale, appended per the struct_size contract. FAAC_VBR_QUALITY_UNSET
(the value faac_params_init writes) means "use quant_quality instead", so
existing callers that set quant_quality keep their exact current behaviour.

Compatibility
-------------

  Library default (faacEncOpen -> LOW) plus quant_quality   byte-identical
  CLI --object-type lc -q N                                 byte-identical
  CLI -b N (ABR, AUTO)                                      byte-identical
  CLI -q N (VBR, AUTO)                                      changes

Only the last row changes, and only because AUTO may now resolve to HE-AAC v1
where it previously chose LC. --object-type lc reproduces the old behaviour
exactly; there is no legacy VBR mode, which would have reinstated the dual code
path this work removed.

For ADTS output this is safe for old decoders: HE-AAC v1 declares profile=LC
with sample_rate=24000 and signals SBR implicitly, so an LC-only decoder plays
the core at the right duration and pitch, just band-limited. MP4 output uses
explicit signalling (profile=HE-AAC), so a decoder that does not know HE-AAC
will refuse it rather than degrade. That is the one surface with residual
legacy risk.


